Twist TODO List
================

  [ ] TELNET
  [ ] RTSP
  [ ] SCP/SFTP, one handler over foxtrot (../foxtrot).  scp:// runs over
      SFTP; there is no SCP wire protocol.  Done: logging in with a key
      file or a password, known_hosts as curl does it (~/.ssh/known_hosts,
      -k skips, no accept-new), a pinned fingerprint, downloading and
      uploading a file to each URL, resuming either way (-C), ranges (-r),
      directory listings, keys in an ssh-agent, keyboard-interactive login
      with the password, and one connection for the URLs of a command
      line.
      Still to do:
        - a transfer that fails, even for a missing file, closes its
          connection, so the next URL logs in again.  An SFTP status
          that leaves the channel sound could keep it.
        - a parked connection that the server closed an instant before
          it is taken fails the transfer with "closed" and does not retry
          on a new one, as curl does.  The command line runs the loop
          once before each URL, which catches closes that have already
          arrived; the window left is a race.
        - nothing parked is sent a disconnect when it is dropped (idle
          for two minutes, or at exit); the TCP connection just closes.
        - no way to forbid reuse: curl has CURLOPT_FORBID_REUSE, and
          twist has neither that nor a tool option for it.
        - ask foxtrot for the number of requests outstanding in an SFTP
          client.  gf_sftp_client_pending is the bytes queued, so twist
          counts its own (sftp.c sent () and inflight).
        - proxies: socks5:// is treated as socks5h:// (the name goes to the
          proxy to look up; curl looks it up itself for socks5://).
          https:// and socks4:// proxies are refused.  Credentials are
          sent for tunnels only: a plain http:// URL through an HTTP
          proxy gets no Proxy-Authorization.  No --noproxy and no proxy
          environment variables.
        - statvfs, and the other commands the sftp tool has, as -Q
          commands; --create-file-mode and the directory mode.
        - quote commands for scp URLs (curl has none either).
        - an HTTP/3 URL does not take a zone: quic_connect reads the host
          with inet_pton itself.  Only the TCP dialers pass the scope.
        - --limit-rate for the other protocols: the pacer is in
          src/engine/rate.c; only the SFTP pumps use it.  Until then the
          command line refuses the option for other URLs.
        - certificates are found only as KEY-cert.pub beside a key file;
          there is no option to name a certificate elsewhere (ssh's
          CertificateFile).
        - -l for ftp URLs, which would send NLST; it only affects sftp
          now
        - -C and -T completion of the URL for the other schemes (curl
          does it for all of them; twist does it for sftp and scp only)
        - FIDO keys held in a file are not supported, and will not be: no
          libfido2.  Keys held by an ssh-agent work, with the agent doing
          the token I/O.
        - keyboard-interactive questions that need a person, such as a
          one-time code: twist answers only a single hidden question
          with the password, as curl does.  A prompt on the terminal
          would need a way for the application to be asked.
      Requires libgcrypt >= 1.12.4.
  [ ] The HTTP pool's key is the loop, scheme, host, port and proxy; it has
      nothing of the TLS settings (verification, CA, client certificate,
      pinning).  A multi handle or share whose handles differ in them can
      use a connection made under others.  The SSH key hashes its
      equivalents (see ssh_pool_key in src/ssh/ssh.c).  The command line is
      unaffected because one operation has one set of options.
  [ ] --connect-timeout covers the greeting and login for SSH only.  The
      other protocols on the shared dialer (SMTP, POP3, IMAP, LDAP, ...) have
      it for the lookup and the TCP connect, and a server that takes the
      connection and sends no greeting is waited for until --max-time.  They
      can call twist_mail_link_phase_begin and _end when they are up.  FTP
      and HTTP dial themselves and do the same.
  [ ] Passphrase-protected OpenSSH-format private keys.  Blocked on
      bcrypt-pbkdf being available in libgcrypt; it is deliberately not
      taken from anywhere else.  An ssh-agent holding the key is the
      workaround, and works.
  [ ] URL globbing is done for URLs and for -o names (#N).  curl also globs
      the file given to -T ({a,b} there, with #N in the URL), and --output-dir
      does not take #N.  Neither is done here: -T takes the name as written.
  [ ] SMB/SMBS
  [ ] RTMP/RTMPS
  [ ] NTLM and Negotiate, only after an explicit dependency decision
  [ ] Brotli.  The coding table can take another name.  Which library,
      and whether a request may use it, is not decided.  Do not
      advertise "br" until it can be decoded.
  [ ] Decide how integration peers are provided.  make check stays
      in-process.  make check-integration dials a peer and skips with
      exit 77 when the peer is absent.  A podman lab is one candidate
      and is not chosen yet.
  [ ] io_uring poll backend
  [ ] Threads and the storage pool.  Found by an audit of the queue, the
      pool and the file handler; none is fixed, and none has been seen
      to fail.  Look at each.
        - storage.c finish_job: if posting the completion to the loop
          fails (an allocation failure), it calls done() on the worker
          thread.  done() is meant to run on the loop, and the handlers
          that use it (the SSH key and known_hosts load) touch the
          transfer, so this is a race.  It needs another way to hand the
          failure back, or a completion that cannot fail to post.
        - file.c file_work: if the final post to the loop fails, the
          worker calls twist_easy_finish itself, a call into the multi
          handle's state from another thread.  The same fix as above
          would cover it.
        - file.c file_free: releasing a transfer waits for the worker,
          which can be in the middle of a read.  On a slow or hung disk
          the loop's thread blocks for that read.  Acceptable for a
          transfer that was let go of; not for one that must be
          cancelled quickly.
        - storage.c twist_storage_set_inline: with it on (nothing turns
          it on now), the file handler runs its worker on the loop's
          thread and deadlocks on any file over four chunks, because
          nothing acknowledges them.  Remove inline mode, or make the
          handler refuse it.
        - pool.c twist_pool_expire: it walks the list without the lock
          and expires any loop's connections, not only the caller's.
          Only the tests call it; real expiry is a timer on each
          connection's own loop.  Remove it, or make it take a loop and
          hold the lock, before anything else calls it.
